      "latency_ms": 2425.1,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 583,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two types of atoms: hydrogen and oxygen. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it is essential:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Three States of Matter\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists in three distinct states depending on the temperature:\n*   **Liquid:** The state we drink, swim in, and use for rain (found in oceans, rivers, and lakes).\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F) (found in glaciers, snow, and ice cubes).\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water heats up and evaporates at or above 100°C (212°F) (found in the air as humidity and in clouds).\n\n### 2. Unique Chemical Properties\nWater has several bizarre and remarkable scientific properties that make it unlike almost any other liquid on Earth:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of this, it easily carries nutrients, chemicals, and minerals wherever it goes.\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense as ice. This is why ice cubes float in a drink, and why lakes freeze from the top down rather than the bottom up, allowing marine life to survive the winter.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps our body temperatures stable.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and to other things (adhesion—which helps water travel up the roots of giant trees).\n\n### 3. Importance to Life\nWater is the bedrock of life as we know it. \n*   **Biology:** About 60% of the adult human body (and roughly 71% of the Earth's surface) is made of water. It cushions our joints, regulates our internal temperature, and transports nutrients through our blood.\n*   **Ecosystems:** All living organisms—from the smallest bacteria to giant blue whales—rely on water to carry out basic cellular processes. Without water, life cannot exist.\n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties that acts as the lifeblood of our planet."
